    "field": "Climate modeling",
    "problem": "This paper addresses the challenge that using computationally cheaper, simplified ocean models may inaccurately represent midlatitude cyclone variability, as decoupling the atmosphere and ocean neglects key interactions like ENSO and can lead to deviations in simulated storm tracks compared to fully coupled models.",
    "method": "The impact of different ocean models on midlatitude cyclones is determined by comparing a fully coupled atmosphere-ocean simulation with experiments using simplified ocean representations (mixed-layer, prescribed SSTs), analyzing cyclone tracks, intensities, and trajectory variability with a cluster approach.",
    "task": "The task is to determine the impact of different and simplified ocean model representations on the simulation of Northern Hemisphere midlatitude cyclone variability by comparing them to a fully coupled atmosphere-ocean model.",
