            "paragraph": "Coupled HOPE-Experiment. ECHAM and HOPE are coupled through OASIS (Ocean Atmosphere Sea-Ice Soil) of Terray et al. (1998) and annual mean flux correction schemes for heat and fresh water; the atmospheric component, ECHAM, is modified compared to the standard version in order to account for sub-grid scale partial ice cover.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ECHAM",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "APPLIES_TO_REALM",
            "target_entity_instance": "atmosphere",
            "target_entity_type": "Realm",
            "paragraph": "Atmosphere-GCM. The atmosphere is simulated by the fourth version of the European Centre model of Hamburg (ECHAM) climate model (Roeckner et al. 1996).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "HOPE",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "APPLIES_TO_REALM",
            "target_entity_instance": "ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "Realm",
            "paragraph": "Ocean-GCM. The dynamic ocean circulation model is the Hamburg ocean model in primitive equations (HOPE) simplified by the Boussinesq approximation and formulated on a Gaussian T42 Arakawa-E grid (Wolff et al. 1997).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ML",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "APPLIES_TO_REALM",
            "target_entity_instance": "ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "Realm",
            "paragraph": "We compare the fully coupled AOGCM ECHAM/HOPE (Legutke and Voss 1999) with ECHAM coupled to a mixed layer (ML) ocean (Karaca and M\u00fcller 1991), ECHAM forced by a varying lower boundary, and ECHAM forced by a climatological lower boundary; the two latter lower boundaries (SST, sea-ice cover, sea-ice thickness, and sea-ice albedo) are derived from the ECHAM/HOPE integration.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ECHAM/HOPE",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "coupled",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "A reference simulation with the coupled atmosphere ocean circulation model ECHAM/HOPE is compared with simulations using ECHAM and three simplified ocean and sea-ice representations: (1) a variable depth mixed layer (ML) ocean, (2) forcing by varying SST and sea-ice, and (3) with climatological SST and sea-ice; the latter two are from the coupled ECHAM/HOPE integration.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ECHAM",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "atmosphere-only",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "Experiment with varying SST. ECHAM is forced by the varying SST, sea-ice cover, sea-ice thickness, and sea-ice albedo obtained by the coupled HOPE-experiment during the reference period. The 99 year simulation contains all physical processes of the atmosphere and the ocean but no atmospheric feedback to the ocean.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ECHAM",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "1000 hPa geopotential height",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "In the present study, the cyclone tracking algorithm of Blender et al. (1997) is applied. Lows are identified as minima of the 1000 hPa geopotential height (z1000) within a neighbourhood of eight grid points.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "cyclone",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Northern Hemisphere",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Most of the Northern Hemispheric cyclones originate in the western ocean basins and move northeastward across the North Atlantic or the North Pacific basins.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "cyclone",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Most of the Northern Hemispheric cyclones originate in the western ocean basins and move northeastward across the North Atlantic or the North Pacific basins.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "cyclone",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Most of the Northern Hemispheric cyclones originate in the western ocean basins and move northeastward across the North Atlantic or the North Pacific basins.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o/Southern Oscillation (ENSO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In the ML- and the climatological simulation, deviations of the mean cyclone distribution are found which occur mainly in the North Pacific, and can partially be traced back to missing El Ni\u00f1o/Southern Oscillation (ENSO) variability.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO)",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Composites of the cyclone intensity for positive minus negative NAO states are shown in Fig. 4a (NAO index above or below one standard deviation). The cyclones reveal the known northward shift in the North Atlantic with the NAO (Sickm\u00f6ller et al. 2000).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
